<p>President Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping concluded talks in Beijing on Friday, ending the first visit to China by a U.S. president since 2017 with statements that emphasized stability but included no confirmed major agreement. Trump claimed broad progress and trade wins, while China’s foreign ministry said the two sides reached “new common understandings”; several accounts said details remained scarce or unconfirmed. Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi later confirmed that Xi would make a state visit to the United States in the autumn at Trump’s invitation, Xi’s first U.S. state visit in more than a decade. Disputes over Taiwan, Iran, trade and advanced technology remained unresolved. Trump told reporters on Air Force One that he had not decided whether to move forward with a major arms package for Taiwan and said he made “no commitment” to Xi on Taiwan; Chinese accounts said Xi warned that mishandling the issue could push the two countries toward “clashes and even conflicts”. On Iran, Trump said Xi agreed Iran should not obtain nuclear weapons, wanted the Strait of Hormuz open and would not send military equipment to Tehran; China publicly urged a lasting ceasefire and the reopening of shipping lanes but did not confirm all of Trump’s account. Trump said China agreed to buy 200 Boeing aircraft and could expand the order to 750. Neither Boeing nor the Chinese government confirmed the purchase agreement or disclosed terms. Technology talks produced no public breakthrough, with reports pointing to unresolved questions over AI cooperation and advanced-chip sales.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/0kVsPUlz0-h5UE50UBzCQFdz4RWu7Vsj9rsR8tUJrzGBSOXYPRvbak0fUEkhBUBbjUyFqBSpoaqUBhfH8tsVXxsnFCHZTtDVMhrlC4KH4BLgvvEdejtxqsxT8yYnRXJYRKiAJPkm-PwSIZ8ShlQMLxLbp4HaVDVxtvpAWapZGVF_m_XZMyU7U3vse9oHNHOFnlTvJU4o4NARqRkwpgwMVGAS' alt='President Trump and China’s leader, Xi Jinping, meeting in Zhongnanhai, a compound used by the Communist Party, in Beijing on Friday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Trade mechanisms: China and the United States agreed to keep implementing previous trade agreements and to establish trade and investment councils, according to Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi.</li><li>Nuclear arms track: Trump said he raised a potential three-way nuclear arms cap with Russia and China, an idea Beijing has previously approached cautiously.</li><li>CEO delegation: Boeing CEO Kelly Ortberg, GE Aerospace CEO H. Lawrence Culp, Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang and other executives joined Trump’s Beijing delegation as aviation, AI chips and market access were on the agenda.</li><li>Rivalry framing: Xi invoked the “Thucydides Trap” during the summit, using the concept of conflict risk between a rising power and an established power while warning on Taiwan.</li></ul><blockquote>We’ve settled a lot of different problems that other people wouldn’t have been able to solve - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>China&#x27;s foreign ministry: The ministry said Xi and Trump agreed to promote steady, sound and sustainable development of China-U.S. relations and bring more peace, prosperity and progress to the world. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/xi-trump-reach-series-of-new-common-understandings-chinas-foreign-ministry/article70981556.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>President Trump: Trump said he was “not looking to have wars” over Taiwan and wanted both Beijing and Taipei to “cool down,” while saying the United States’ position had not changed. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ce8p61v7l68o'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>U.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio: Rubio said U.S. policy on Taiwan remained “unchanged” after Xi’s warning over the island. (<a href='https://theweek.com/world-news/xi-warning-summit-trump'>The Week</a>)</li><li>Boeing CEO Kelly Ortberg: Before the trip, Ortberg told analysts that any China order was “100% dependent” on U.S.-China negotiations and relations. (<a href='https: